# Form eyebrow ≠ placeholder

An inset **`eyebrow`** is the field name that stays visible after the user types. The
**`placeholder`** is empty-state hint text. They must never be the same string, the same
catalog key, or the same user-facing words with only punctuation changed (`Filter` /
`Filter...`).

## Do

- Keep the eyebrow as the field title (`Email`, `RSS feed URL`, `Username`).
- Give the placeholder a different role:
  - an obvious **example** (`you@example.com`, `https://example.com/feed.xml`)
  - **`Optional`** / **`Required`** (`misc.optional` / `misc.required`)
  - the **default if left blank** (`Anonymous`)
  - a short **instruction** (`At least 8 characters`, `Type your email`, `Type a title`)
- Confirm-password may reuse the password placeholder while keeping a distinct eyebrow
  (`Confirm password` + `Password` / password hint).

## Don't

- Don't pass the same `t(...)` key (or the same literal) to both `eyebrow` and
  `placeholder`.
- Don't invent a near-duplicate of the eyebrow (`Filter` / `Filter...`).

## Related

- **management-web-form-eyebrow** — use `eyebrow` instead of a freestanding `Label`
- **mobile-screen-layout** / **mobile-reusable-components** — forms pass both props;
  search and list filters omit `eyebrow`
- **ui-copy-sentence-case** — placeholders are user-facing copy
